GIMP Deskew Plug-In
===================

This fork exists to distribute a precompiled Flatpak extension of Deskew 1.2
for GIMP 3.

The plug-in and its deskewing functionality are based entirely on the original
upstream source code. The Flatpak packaging was created with AI-assisted
guidance.

This is an unofficial community build.


Usage
=====

Open an image and click:

    Layer => Transform => Deskew

The plug-in has no options. GIMP will automatically attempt to straighten
the active layer.

If Deskew does not work, make sure the image is in RGB mode and remove the
alpha channel from the active layer first:

    Layer => Transparency => Remove Alpha Channel


Flatpak Installation
====================

This package is intended for GIMP 3 installed through Flatpak.

Download the following file from the Releases page:

    org.gimp.GIMP.Plugin.Deskew.flatpak

Deskew should be installed in the same Flatpak scope as GIMP.

Check whether GIMP is installed for the current user:

    flatpak list --user | grep org.gimp.GIMP

Check whether GIMP is installed system-wide:

    flatpak list --system | grep org.gimp.GIMP


User Installation
=================

If GIMP is installed for the current user, run:

    flatpak install --user ./org.gimp.GIMP.Plugin.Deskew.flatpak


System-Wide Installation
========================

If GIMP is installed system-wide, run:

    sudo flatpak install --system ./org.gimp.GIMP.Plugin.Deskew.flatpak


Restarting GIMP
===============

After installation, restart GIMP completely:

    flatpak kill org.gimp.GIMP
    flatpak run org.gimp.GIMP

Deskew should appear under:

    Layer => Transform => Deskew


Uninstallation
==============

Remove a user installation with:

    flatpak uninstall --user org.gimp.GIMP.Plugin.Deskew

Remove a system-wide installation with:

    sudo flatpak uninstall --system org.gimp.GIMP.Plugin.Deskew


Building from Source
====================

If you downloaded the source code from Git:

    ./autogen.sh
    make
    make install

If you downloaded a release tarball:

    ./configure
    make
    make install

Both ./autogen.sh and ./configure support a custom installation prefix:

    ./configure --prefix=INSTALL_PATH

A typical native Linux installation path is /usr. The default is /usr/local.


